WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.283
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.045 --> 00:00:07.670
ADRIAN MACNEIL: Good
morning, everyone.

00:00:07.670 --> 00:00:10.250
Welcome and thanks
for joining us today.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:11.530
My name is Adrian Macneil.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.150
And I'm a director
of engineering

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.420
at Cruise Automation.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.030
So at Cruise I lead our
engineering productivity team.

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.190
And so we're responsible
for building tools

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.820
and infrastructure that
supports the unique development

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:29.070
workflows of building
a self-driving car.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.660
If you're not
familiar with Cruise,

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:33.413
we're a self-driving car
startup here in San Francisco.

00:00:33.413 --> 00:00:34.830
So we're backed
by General Motors.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:39.090
We have also raised around $5
billion in additional funding

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.520
from SoftBank and Honda.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.690
And our mission is to launch
a fully driverless car.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.480
So if you're a passenger
in one of our cars,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.960
there's no need for you to be
responsible for the driving

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.957
at all.

00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:52.540
You're going to-- you don't need
to pay attention to the road.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.248
You can sit back.

00:00:53.248 --> 00:00:53.820
You can eat.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.090
You can read a book, watch
a movie, get some work done,

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.280
or sleep if you want.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.460
And our vehicles are powered
by a wide variety of sensors.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:06.480
So we have cameras, LIDARs,
several different types

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.800
of radars, and also some
powerful onboard computers.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.650
And our car needs to use
all of these sensors to--

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.000
first of all, it needs to
make a 3D model of the world.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.610
So it needs to understand
its environment.

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.110
The second thing
is it needs to make

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.670
a plan for how it
wants to navigate

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.780
through this environment.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:29.260
And finally, it needs to turn
this plan into the steering,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.330
braking, acceleration commands.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.810
So this is the control stack.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.670
We're currently testing
our vehicles here

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.830
on the streets of San Francisco.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.290
And probably one of the best
perks about working for Cruise

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.207
is that we have this
mobile app that employees

00:01:43.207 --> 00:01:46.390
can use to request rides
in self-driving cars

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.440
anywhere in the city.

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.320
And testing in this
complex urban environment

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.150
provides really interesting,
incredibly rich data

00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:59.690
that our cars and our
engineers can learn from.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:01.858
What really excites
me at Cruise is

00:02:01.858 --> 00:02:03.650
getting to work on
tools and infrastructure

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.500
that support the unique
development workflows

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.360
of our self-driving cars.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.639
So this actually is
an example of one

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:12.380
of our internal
tools called WebVis.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.660
And it's a 3D web-based
visualization tool

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.410
that allows our engineers to
understand any of our driving

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.960
sensor data, see exactly
what the car was thinking,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.138
or seeing, or doing
at any point in time.

00:02:23.138 --> 00:02:25.430
There's different panels that
they can turn on and off,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.990
and different
overlays to understand

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.370
the internals of the AV stack.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.590
This talk is specifically
about our simulation at Cruise.

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:37.068
And simulation is incredibly
important for building

00:02:37.068 --> 00:02:37.860
a self-driving car.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.510
It's one of the most
important pillars.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.353
And it's also a super
interesting challenge.

00:02:41.353 --> 00:02:43.020
So first of all, I'm
going to talk a bit

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.450
about what I mean
by a simulation

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.923
for self-driving cars
and why that's important.

00:02:47.923 --> 00:02:50.340
And then we're going to talk
about the infrastructure that

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.865
allows us to run all of
these simulations at scale.

00:02:53.865 --> 00:02:55.740
And finally, I'm going
to talk about the data

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.300
pipeline and
analytics that allow

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.610
us to understand
all of this data

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:01.950
that we get from
our simulations.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.140
So first of all, why is
simulation important?

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.880
As I mentioned, we
test out our cars

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:08.300
on the streets of San Francisco.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.700
And it's a super
challenging environment.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:14.650
We encounter pedestrians
and cyclists, motorbikes,

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:17.770
double-parked cars, emergency
vehicles, construction zones

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.960
far more frequently
than we would

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:22.870
if we were testing our cars--
or when we test our cars in more

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:24.870
suburban environments.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.800
And I'm going to talk a bit more
about this photo in a minute.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.175
But first of all, I want to
show you a couple of examples

00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:32.020
of the types of
situations that we

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.477
run into on a daily basis,
driving in San Francisco.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.080
So the way our cars are
able to learn so quickly

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.650
is that we do things like this.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.590
We take them in San
Francisco into the middle

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:52.905
of a school zone at rush hour.

00:03:52.905 --> 00:03:54.280
And this is the
kind of situation

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.950
that's difficult even
for a human to navigate.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:03:59.650
So it gives us
incredibly useful data

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.360
about not only how our cars
behave in this situation,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:09.760
but also this is data that we
can train from and learn from.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.622
We run into construction
zones pretty frequently.

00:04:12.622 --> 00:04:15.080
So our car needs to be able to
handle a sort of dynamically

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.018
changing city environment.

00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:24.530
Double-parked cars are a
very frequent occurrence

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.040
in San Francisco.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.490
Even moving double-parked
cars in this case.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.323
And I guess you can't see
here, but we're actually

00:04:32.323 --> 00:04:33.240
stopping for a-- yeah.

00:04:38.928 --> 00:04:40.310
And sometimes,
when we're getting

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:41.240
round double-parked
cars, we need

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.490
to come into oncoming traffic.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:47.000
And we also need to understand
when people are yielding for us

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.900
and sort of expecting us to go.

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:53.156
And these are all things that we
got through fully autonomously.

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:58.540
Finally, when we're making
unprotected left turns,

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.850
we need to know how to
look for a gap in traffic

00:05:00.850 --> 00:05:04.580
and how to clear
an intersection.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.930
So in the early days of Cruise,
our development workflow mostly

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.508
consisted of taking
an example like this,

00:05:10.508 --> 00:05:12.550
one of these scenarios,
and maybe something where

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.560
we thought we could have
done a better job at.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.227
One of our engineers
would download this

00:05:16.227 --> 00:05:17.355
to their computer.

00:05:17.355 --> 00:05:18.980
They would be a run
through it locally,

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:22.930
maybe make some code changes,
or introduce a new ML model.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.870
And then they would run it
through the CI and unit tests.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.070
And once they thought
that that was successful,

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:29.623
they would deploy
it on the road.

00:05:29.623 --> 00:05:31.540
And we would do that to
gather additional data

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:35.210
about how that code is
performing in production.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:38.422
But this is a pretty inefficient
workflow for two reasons.

00:05:38.422 --> 00:05:40.130
First of all, it takes
a really long time

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:42.392
to get feedback
to our engineers.

00:05:42.392 --> 00:05:44.850
If every time we want to know
how our driving is improving,

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.670
we need to go out and
deploy it to a car,

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:48.503
drive around the road,
and just sort of hope

00:05:48.503 --> 00:05:52.442
we run into more challenging
situations like this.

00:05:52.442 --> 00:05:54.400
The second thing is, I
guess, most importantly,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.610
if an engineer is only
working on a single scenario

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.950
locally and then making
changes to that code,

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:02.490
they may have made that one
scenario significantly better,

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.198
but they don't know
if they've made three

00:06:04.198 --> 00:06:05.640
other scenarios slightly worse.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.130
So it's really important
that our engineers

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.120
can test their code across
a wide variety of scenarios

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:16.458
and sort of understand
how our behavior is.

00:06:16.458 --> 00:06:17.750
I'll give you one more example.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:19.425
So this is us-- it
starts with us making

00:06:19.425 --> 00:06:20.530
an unprotected left turn.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:21.947
We've got pedestrians
that we need

00:06:21.947 --> 00:06:24.240
to yield to and look for a gap.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.751
And then we come across
a construction zone.

00:06:27.751 --> 00:06:30.220
And we need to identify that
this construction worker here

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:35.560
is holding up the stop sign,
that he's expecting us to wait.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.570
And then once he sort
of starts waving us on,

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:40.440
it kind of needs to
navigate through the scene.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:42.130
And it also needs
to check that it's

00:06:42.130 --> 00:06:43.900
safe to cross these
double yellow lines,

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:46.150
and check that we're not
worried about that other guys

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:47.608
sort of running
out in front of us.

00:06:47.608 --> 00:06:50.430
Why is he standing on the road?

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.430
And this is another
example of why simulation

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:55.640
is really important, because
while we run into-- we come

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:59.340
across construction zones
every day in San Francisco,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.340
no two construction
zones are the same.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.715
And it's really
important that we

00:07:02.715 --> 00:07:04.820
can understand not only
how our car behaves

00:07:04.820 --> 00:07:07.310
in scenarios that we
have seen, but also ones

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:08.330
that we haven't seen.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:10.730
And so with simulation, we
can take something like this,

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:13.805
and we can ask questions like,
what would have happened if--

00:07:13.805 --> 00:07:16.430
what if this human was standing
more further out into the road?

00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.433
What if the cones were
in a different place?

00:07:18.433 --> 00:07:19.850
What if the
construction equipment

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:21.150
was in a different place?

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:24.620
So we can take one of
these scenarios and we

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:25.970
can create many--

00:07:25.970 --> 00:07:30.340
potentially hundreds or
thousands of variations on it.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:31.750
So why is simulation important?

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.020
It allows us to validate
our driving behavior

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:37.110
across a large database
of different examples

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.990
before we go
driving on the road.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:42.580
We get a faster feedback
cycle for our engineers.

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.990
And we can understand how
our behavior changes as we

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:46.590
vary one of those scenarios.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:50.380
I'm going to talk a little
bit more about that later.

00:07:50.380 --> 00:07:52.560
So this brings us back
to the intersection

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:53.640
with the cable car.

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:56.310
This photo was not actually
taken in San Francisco.

00:07:56.310 --> 00:07:58.830
This is from our simulated
driving environment

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:00.840
known internally as the Matrix.

00:08:00.840 --> 00:08:03.630
And just like the
Matrix, the car

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:05.280
has no idea that it's inside.

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.340
So we are simulating
everything from the camera,

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:10.980
LIDAR inputs using ray tracing.

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.410
And then the car goes
through its normal process

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:15.180
of doing perception,
planning, controls,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.180
and responding with steering
and braking commands.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:20.460
And testing in a simulated
environment like this

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:24.360
allows us to reliably reproduce
weird edge cases and scenarios

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.748
that we haven't seen
in the real world.

00:08:27.748 --> 00:08:29.290
In a simulated
environment like this,

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:33.929
we can control everything;
pedestrians, cars, cable cars,

00:08:33.929 --> 00:08:35.730
even other Cruise cars.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.200
And so simulation
like this is one

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:40.460
of the most important pillars
of self-driving car development.

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.450
We've got a bit of work to do
on our pedestrians walking.

00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:53.280
So this is just one type
of example of simulation.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.600
So this is what's known as an
end-to-end simulation, where

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.880
we're simulating everything
from our sensory input

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:00.950
through to the steering
and braking commands.

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:03.200
But there's many other
different types of simulations.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:04.940
So another example
is a replay test,

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.323
where we're taking
actual sensor data

00:09:07.323 --> 00:09:08.990
that we've recorded
from the real world,

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:11.240
playing that back
against the car stack,

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:13.400
and then we can compare
the results of this

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:14.810
to our steering.

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.490
We can compare the
results of what

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.510
we detected to human-labeled
ground truth data.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:21.320
And that allows us
to score how well

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:22.790
the car did at
turning that sensor

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.954
input into an accurate 3D
model of its environment.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.750
Another example was a
planning simulation.

00:09:30.750 --> 00:09:34.067
So in a planning simulation,
we can construct an example

00:09:34.067 --> 00:09:35.150
like an intersection here.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.740
We have some pedestrians
entering a crosswalk.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:39.410
The car is trying
to make a left turn.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.740
And we can create an assertion
such as that the car must

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:45.660
yield to these pedestrians.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.480
And the key difference with
this type of simulation

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.640
is that we're not simulating
sensor input here.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:54.565
We're simply giving the car
a known state of the world,

00:09:54.565 --> 00:09:56.190
and then we're
validating its behavior.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:57.600
And this is much
cheaper to run if we

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.640
want to do hundreds or
thousands of variations.

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:03.730
This is not an exhaustive list.

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:06.188
There's many other different
types of tests and simulations

00:10:06.188 --> 00:10:06.837
we run.

00:10:06.837 --> 00:10:09.420
So for example, we also do a lot
of performance testing, where

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:13.200
we're running our car stack
against real car compute

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.460
hardware.

00:10:14.460 --> 00:10:16.920
And that way we can benchmark
things like latency, CPU,

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:20.460
memory, GPU use.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:23.797
So we have a huge number of
these different scenarios.

00:10:23.797 --> 00:10:25.380
Every one of these
scenarios, we might

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:27.540
want to create hundreds
or potentially thousands

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.520
of different variations.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.678
Each of these tests that we run
requires large runtime assets.

00:10:33.678 --> 00:10:35.220
So sometimes, you
might be using over

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.050
100 gigs of data that needs
to be copied onto that worker.

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:42.990
And that's things like binary
assets, map tiles, ML models,

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:45.620
recorded sensor data.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:48.760
And finally, these
tests produce really

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.230
detailed matrix and data.

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:54.100
So software in car simulation
is much more than just

00:10:54.100 --> 00:10:56.522
a pass/fail result,
just like driving well

00:10:56.522 --> 00:10:58.480
on the road is much more
than just not crashing

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:00.200
or just following
the road rules.

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:03.250
So our engineers really need to
understand exactly how our car

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:07.450
behaves in that simulation.

00:11:07.450 --> 00:11:10.360
It's critical that we pair the
simulation with on-road driving

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:11.812
for a couple of reasons.

00:11:11.812 --> 00:11:14.020
First of all, we want to do
on-road driving so we can

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:17.142
find new and unique situations.

00:11:17.142 --> 00:11:18.850
And the second one is
we need to validate

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.630
that our simulations accurately
reflect the types of things

00:11:22.630 --> 00:11:24.980
that we're observing
in the real world.

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:28.180
So by combining this on-road
driving in complex urban

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:31.840
environments with the simulation
maximizes the result--

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.180
the rate at which
our cars can learn.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.430
So that gives you a sort
of a brief understanding

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.542
of what simulation means
and why it's important.

00:11:40.542 --> 00:11:42.750
Next, I'm going to talk
about the infrastructure that

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:47.320
allows us to run these
simulations at scale.

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:50.360
Testing infrastructure at Cruise
is running at massive scale.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:54.490
So every day we're running over
200,000 hours of simulations.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:57.880
We do this on a cluster of
30,000 instances with 5,000

00:11:57.880 --> 00:11:58.840
GPUs.

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:02.170
And you can think about this
as 30,000 virtual cars driving

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:05.830
around every day to
validate our cars' behavior.

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:07.920
And just to put that
number in perspective,

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.450
Cruise has around
180 test vehicles

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:12.500
registered with the
state of California.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:16.180
So we're running over 100
times more cars in simulation

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:19.030
than we do in the real world.

00:12:19.030 --> 00:12:22.680
We also produce nearly a third
of a petabyte a day of results

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:23.970
from all of these simulations.

00:12:26.575 --> 00:12:28.450
If you asked me a year
ago, whether I thought

00:12:28.450 --> 00:12:30.090
we were running
large-scale testing

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:33.100
infrastructure at Cruise, I
would have said, absolutely.

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.740
But the use of
simulation at Cruise

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:37.660
is rapidly expanding
as our engineers

00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:41.650
find new and unique ways to
benefit from these simulations.

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:44.170
And so our test
infrastructure and the number

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.570
of hours of compute
that we've been running

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:49.600
has grown by over 25 times
in the past 12 months alone.

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.970
So you can imagine this provides
some pretty interesting scaling

00:12:55.970 --> 00:12:56.548
challenges.

00:12:56.548 --> 00:12:58.340
So we have really rapid
scheduling of jobs.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:00.230
Sometimes, jobs
are changing state

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.210
over 1,000 times per second.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:04.015
We need to deal with
CPU, GPU resource

00:13:04.015 --> 00:13:05.390
availability to
make sure we have

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:08.083
quotas to support our demand.

00:13:08.083 --> 00:13:09.500
We need to balance
our utilization

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.083
and make sure that we don't have
too many workers sitting idle

00:13:12.083 --> 00:13:14.280
or that we don't
have jobs queueing.

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:15.980
We need to distribute
these job inputs.

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:17.880
100 gigabytes of data
needs to efficiently

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:20.790
be moved to potentially
thousands of machines

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.590
quickly and efficiently.

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:24.420
And finally, we
need a data pipeline

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.090
that allows us to make
sense of all of this data

00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:30.900
that we generate
from the simulations.

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:32.450
We choose to run
this in Google Cloud

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.910
because it allows us to
scale much faster than we can

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:36.780
on our own physical
infrastructure.

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:38.990
So we do have some
on-prem hardware

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:41.300
that we use for testing on
real car compute hardware.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.860
As I mentioned, that's great
for performance benchmarking.

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:48.340
But the vast majority of our
compute runs in Google Cloud.

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:49.840
One of the other
main advantages is

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:52.690
that Google supports these
custom machine worker types.

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:56.150
So we can choose exactly how
many GPUs, how many CPU cores,

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:58.040
and how much RAM we
want for an instance.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:13:59.957
And this is really
valuable when we're running

00:13:59.957 --> 00:14:02.130
different kinds of simulations.

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:04.877
We get access to the latest
generation GPUs and hardware.

00:14:04.877 --> 00:14:06.960
And this is really beneficial
for the productivity

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:08.580
of our engineers.

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:11.340
And we're able to use the best
in class managed services.

00:14:11.340 --> 00:14:14.953
So we take advantage of
Spinnaker, Pub/Sub, BigQuery,

00:14:14.953 --> 00:14:15.995
and Google Cloud Storage.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.427
I guess to-- before we talk
about our current architecture,

00:14:22.427 --> 00:14:24.510
it's helpful to think about
where we've come from.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:27.780
So in 2016, Cruise was
a pretty small company.

00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:31.110
When it was acquired originally,
it was about 40 employees.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:34.350
And we treated simulation
as fundamentally part of CI.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:37.740
So we had a CI system that
launched VMs and containers.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.870
And the CI system had a
homogeneous worker pool.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:42.600
So we had to run all
of our builds and tests

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:45.440
on expensive GPU instances.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.550
We were also limited to
16 times parallelization.

00:14:48.550 --> 00:14:50.550
And that was due to some
performance bottlenecks

00:14:50.550 --> 00:14:51.670
in the system.

00:14:51.670 --> 00:14:53.700
So ultimately, this
resulted in engineers

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:58.680
spending a lot of time waiting
for simulations to run.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:01.650
We solved this by introducing
a new tool called Hydra.

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:03.360
And Hydra is a job scheduler.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:06.480
So it's called by a CI or
users requesting a job.

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:10.020
It consists of a master, and
then various worker pools.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:11.820
And it can send jobs
to these worker pools.

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:14.750
On the worker, we use
Docker to execute the job.

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:17.700
And on the GPU workers, we're
using Nvidia Docker to provide

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:20.410
access to that hardware.

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:23.290
The Hydra master writes
all of its job state

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:25.480
and kind of results
of these simulations

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:27.465
into a postgres database.

00:15:27.465 --> 00:15:29.590
And then as often happens
at early stage companies,

00:15:29.590 --> 00:15:32.007
we ended up with a whole bunch
of dashboards and reporting

00:15:32.007 --> 00:15:35.300
tools, which were talking
directly to that database.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:37.287
As our load increased
though, it became clear

00:15:37.287 --> 00:15:39.620
that this architecture was
not going to scale very well.

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:41.662
So there were performance
bottlenecks, especially

00:15:41.662 --> 00:15:44.220
in the database with
locking and triggers.

00:15:44.220 --> 00:15:48.120
And it was clear that we
needed to revise this.

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:51.010
For anyone who's made change
to a production system before,

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:54.600
though, you'll know that it's
much easier said than done.

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.510
So any API that
can be depended on

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:58.990
would probably be
depended on somewhere.

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:00.950
And we had things like
various reporting tools

00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:03.020
that were talking directly
to these databases.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:04.580
We had triggers that we
didn't want to interrupt.

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:06.330
We wanted to switch
over to the new system

00:16:06.330 --> 00:16:08.240
without any downtime.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.867
And we wanted to keep the
worker API backwards compatible.

00:16:13.462 --> 00:16:15.170
So at the start of
this year, we migrated

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.320
to our new architecture.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.370
And Hydra 2 is a collection
of individually scalable

00:16:19.370 --> 00:16:20.480
components.

00:16:20.480 --> 00:16:24.157
So we completely rewrote
the Hydra master in Go.

00:16:24.157 --> 00:16:25.490
And that manages the scheduling.

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:29.270
And it talks via the exact same
API, backwards compatible API,

00:16:29.270 --> 00:16:30.920
to our workers.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:32.470
The scheduler has
its own database.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:34.560
So it's using Cloud
Spanner to track

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.990
all of the current
in-progress job state.

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.270
And then from there, we
replicate the job execution

00:16:39.270 --> 00:16:42.240
history, first of all, back
to that reporting database

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.310
that tools were depending on.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.230
And then also through
Pub/Sub to BigQuery,

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:51.030
which is a much more
scalable data warehouse.

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:52.650
Hydra 2 was built
to take advantage

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:54.192
of all of the building
blocks that we

00:16:54.192 --> 00:16:55.982
have available to us in GCP.

00:16:55.982 --> 00:16:58.440
So we're using things like
Cloud Load Balancing, Kubernetes

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.900
Engine, and Cloud Spanner
to run the master.

00:17:00.900 --> 00:17:03.330
We're using Container
Registry and Cloud Storage

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:05.040
for storing our inputs.

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:07.230
We have output data
going into Cloud Storage.

00:17:07.230 --> 00:17:09.630
And then we're also using
Pub/Sub and BigQuery

00:17:09.630 --> 00:17:14.305
to manage our output data
and our execution logs.

00:17:14.305 --> 00:17:16.430
I'm going to talk through
each of these components.

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:18.972
But we're going to start with
the most interesting one, which

00:17:18.972 --> 00:17:20.250
is the worker pools.

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:22.530
So our worker pools are
made up of tens of thousands

00:17:22.530 --> 00:17:23.550
of instances.

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:26.550
And our goal here is
basically to recreate

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:28.560
this, an entire
room of servers that

00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:34.470
are running for our benefit,
from the comfort of our laptop.

00:17:34.470 --> 00:17:37.095
We do this using Google Compute
Engine, which is great product.

00:17:37.095 --> 00:17:38.955
It allows us to spin
up a VM on demand.

00:17:38.955 --> 00:17:40.830
And as I mentioned, one
of the great features

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:42.450
about Compute Engine
is that we can

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:48.530
choose exactly what ratios of
GPU, CPU, and RAM that we want.

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:51.842
We're not limited to these kind
of predefined instance sizes.

00:17:51.842 --> 00:17:53.300
And so Compute
Engine has a feature

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:54.550
called Manage Instance Groups.

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:58.130
And this is similar to
autoscaling groups in AWS.

00:17:58.130 --> 00:18:00.230
And this allows you to
create a template instance.

00:18:00.230 --> 00:18:01.605
And then you can
scale up or down

00:18:01.605 --> 00:18:07.410
many, many hundreds of instances
of the exact same size.

00:18:07.410 --> 00:18:09.540
Managed Instance Groups
come in two flavors.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:11.790
So there's Regional
Managed Instance Groups,

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:15.330
and these are tied to a
specific region, such as US-West

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.000
And they automatically scale
up instances and balance them

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:19.390
across all of the zones.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.660
So in this example, we
have US-West as our region.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:23.590
There's an instance group in it.

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:25.710
And then we're creating
all of the instances

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:29.310
in the three zones in US-West.

00:18:29.310 --> 00:18:31.890
Unfortunately, when you use
Regional Managed Instance

00:18:31.890 --> 00:18:34.050
Groups, it's very
aggressive at making sure

00:18:34.050 --> 00:18:36.180
that you have balance of
the number of instances

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:37.690
across the different zones.

00:18:37.690 --> 00:18:40.230
So this is primarily designed
for high availability

00:18:40.230 --> 00:18:41.112
workloads.

00:18:41.112 --> 00:18:42.570
If you're serving
customer traffic,

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:45.068
you want to have spare
capacity and make sure

00:18:45.068 --> 00:18:47.610
that if requests are coming in,
you don't end up dropping one

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.430
just because a zone went down.

00:18:49.430 --> 00:18:52.260
But for batch workloads, we
don't actually care about that.

00:18:52.260 --> 00:18:54.900
We do care about,
though, the fact that--

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.030
in this example, I
guess, if we scale down

00:18:57.030 --> 00:19:00.747
a couple of instances
in us-west1-a,

00:19:00.747 --> 00:19:02.580
we don't want it to
automatically just start

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:04.740
killing random
instances in us-west-c

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:06.720
and start up new ones
in a just to maintain

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.940
that perfect balance.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:11.140
If we're halfway through a
compute job, for example,

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:13.890
we might be halfway through
a 20 or 30-minute job,

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:15.390
and we really don't
want it to start

00:19:15.390 --> 00:19:17.890
killing random instances that
are halfway through those jobs

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:20.538
just so that we could
have perfect balance.

00:19:20.538 --> 00:19:22.330
So the other option
that you have available

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:24.610
are the Zonal Managed
Instance Groups.

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:26.450
And in a Zonal Managed
Instance Group,

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:28.920
it is only scaling independently
within a single zone.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.060
So we can create one
instance group for each zone,

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:32.810
and we don't need to
worry about perfectly

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:36.310
balancing these workers.

00:19:36.310 --> 00:19:38.110
Another challenge
that we have here

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:39.670
is that these
instance groups are

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:44.373
limited to running 1,000
instances per group.

00:19:44.373 --> 00:19:45.790
And so if you talk
to Google, it's

00:19:45.790 --> 00:19:47.830
possible to increase
this limit a little bit,

00:19:47.830 --> 00:19:50.980
but not sufficient to run the
tens of thousands of instances

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:52.810
that we want.

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:56.333
So the way we solved this is
by implementing Sharded Managed

00:19:56.333 --> 00:19:57.000
Instance Groups.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.250
Basically, in each zone, we
would create more than one

00:19:59.250 --> 00:20:00.500
Managed Instance Group.

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:03.750
And that allows us to
scale above 1,000 workers

00:20:03.750 --> 00:20:06.070
in a single zone.

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:08.420
This does make autoscaling
a bit more challenging.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:12.600
I'm going to talk a bit more
about autoscaling in a second.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.330
Each of these Managed
Instance Groups

00:20:14.330 --> 00:20:16.728
creates an instance
from a single template.

00:20:16.728 --> 00:20:18.770
So if you want to run
different types of workers,

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:20.810
like CPU and GPU
workers, you need

00:20:20.810 --> 00:20:22.730
to create more than
one instance group.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.132
At Cruise, we also
have different types--

00:20:25.132 --> 00:20:27.090
different size instances
for each one of these.

00:20:27.090 --> 00:20:30.290
So on our CPU workers, some
jobs only need a few cores,

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:33.920
and some jobs can take
advantage of 96-core machines.

00:20:33.920 --> 00:20:36.470
Likewise, some of our GPU
jobs only need one GPU.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:38.790
Others can take advantage
of multiple GPUs.

00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:40.580
And we obviously want
to optimize the cost

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:43.360
by not running workloads
on machines that are

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.890
overprovisioned for that job.

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:48.640
So bringing this
back to our diagram,

00:20:48.640 --> 00:20:51.090
we now have multiple
availability zones

00:20:51.090 --> 00:20:51.690
in the region.

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:55.960
Each of the availability zones
has multiple instance types.

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:57.510
And then each of
these instance types

00:20:57.510 --> 00:20:59.710
has multiple managed
instance groups within it.

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:01.560
So at this point, we're starting
to get quite a few managed

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:02.910
instance groups up here.

00:21:02.910 --> 00:21:06.630
But there's one more thing
that we need to consider.

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:09.578
And the final picture of
the piece of the puzzle

00:21:09.578 --> 00:21:11.370
is which region to
select for the workload.

00:21:11.370 --> 00:21:12.950
So it's a little bit--

00:21:12.950 --> 00:21:15.117
it's actually a map of the
world, believe it or not.

00:21:16.793 --> 00:21:18.210
And if you're
thinking about where

00:21:18.210 --> 00:21:20.587
to run your compute workloads,
there are several factors

00:21:20.587 --> 00:21:21.670
that you need to consider.

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:23.910
So you need to, obviously, think
about legal considerations,

00:21:23.910 --> 00:21:25.452
if there are things
like data privacy

00:21:25.452 --> 00:21:27.900
and where your data is
being stored and processed;

00:21:27.900 --> 00:21:30.510
latency requirements, if your
jobs need to talk to data,

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:33.793
talk to each other or talk to
data in a different region.

00:21:33.793 --> 00:21:35.835
You want to co-locate your
data and your compute.

00:21:35.835 --> 00:21:38.750
So you don't want all of
your data stored in US-West

00:21:38.750 --> 00:21:41.360
and then your compute
happening in US-East.

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:43.820
And finally, not all
regions are created equal.

00:21:43.820 --> 00:21:46.070
So if you're doing things
like this, running thousands

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:49.113
of GPUs, you really want to
talk with your TAM or solutions

00:21:49.113 --> 00:21:51.530
architect and get a sense of
which region is going to best

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:54.710
support those workloads.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:57.530
In our case, the answer
was multiple regions.

00:21:57.530 --> 00:22:00.520
So generally, when people are
running in multiple regions,

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:03.533
they're doing this, again,
for high availability reasons.

00:22:03.533 --> 00:22:05.950
But in our case, we care more
about having the flexibility

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:08.200
that we can run our
workloads in whichever region

00:22:08.200 --> 00:22:09.950
is easiest to get capacity.

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:12.498
So we have pretty spiky
workloads during the day.

00:22:12.498 --> 00:22:14.290
Sometimes, an engineer
will kick off a job,

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:17.440
and we might need
thousands of more servers.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.318
And being able to create
that in any region

00:22:19.318 --> 00:22:21.610
gives us great flexibility
to be able to take advantage

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:22.975
of quotas in multiple regions.

00:22:25.755 --> 00:22:27.880
Running in multiple regions,
of course, isn't free.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:29.588
So there's additional
cost and complexity

00:22:29.588 --> 00:22:31.050
with setting this up.

00:22:31.050 --> 00:22:32.393
You need to think about latency.

00:22:32.393 --> 00:22:34.810
For example, if you are running
machine learning training,

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:36.435
you would not want
your workers talking

00:22:36.435 --> 00:22:38.185
across regions, because
that's going to be

00:22:38.185 --> 00:22:39.508
quite a lot of extra latency.

00:22:39.508 --> 00:22:41.050
But for simulation
workloads, they're

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:42.425
all running pretty
independently.

00:22:42.425 --> 00:22:45.010
So that wasn't a problem for us.

00:22:45.010 --> 00:22:46.760
You do need to think
carefully about where

00:22:46.760 --> 00:22:48.248
your data is located.

00:22:48.248 --> 00:22:50.540
As I mentioned, you don't
want your data stored in West

00:22:50.540 --> 00:22:52.010
and your compute
happening in East.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:54.230
So Google Cloud Storage
has this great feature

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:58.847
called multi-regional buckets.

00:22:58.847 --> 00:23:00.680
And when you create a
multi-regional bucket,

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:03.500
you choose a
continent, like the US,

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.030
and then it
automatically replicates

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:08.933
your data across the different
regions in that continent.

00:23:08.933 --> 00:23:10.600
So when you use a
multi-regional bucket,

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:13.110
you pay slightly
more for the storage,

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.370
but then it makes transfer
within all of those regions

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:18.480
free and fast.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:20.940
So bringing this back
to our little diagram,

00:23:20.940 --> 00:23:23.495
we have at the top some
multi-regional buckets.

00:23:23.495 --> 00:23:25.620
So we're using Cloud Storage
and Container Registry

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:26.910
for storing our data.

00:23:26.910 --> 00:23:28.680
We then have multiple regions.

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:31.740
Each one of these regions has
multiple availability zones.

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.240
Each one of these
availability zones

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:34.523
has multiple worker types.

00:23:34.523 --> 00:23:35.940
And each one of
these worker types

00:23:35.940 --> 00:23:38.800
has multiple instance
groups associated with it.

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:40.692
So we really end up
with a huge number.

00:23:40.692 --> 00:23:42.150
You can see how
it's easy to end up

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:45.523
with dozens of Managed
Instance Groups doing this.

00:23:45.523 --> 00:23:47.690
To manage all of this, we're
huge fans of Terraform.

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:49.550
So Terraform allows
us to easily define

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:50.890
the infrastructure as code.

00:23:50.890 --> 00:23:53.950
And we can easily make updates
and roll out changes to this.

00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:55.910
And Terraform modules
are a really great way

00:23:55.910 --> 00:23:58.278
to build something like
this, because we can set up

00:23:58.278 --> 00:24:00.320
a template for this is
what a particular instance

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:01.250
group should look like.

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:02.510
And then you can
use that to compose

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:03.500
what a zone should look like.

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:05.810
And you can use that to compose
what a region should look like.

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:07.940
And you can sort of
template and parameterize

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:11.370
each one of those modules.

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:13.590
When we run this
many instance groups,

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:17.580
we need to think carefully about
how and where we are scaling.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.710
So a typical workday
at Cruise starts

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:22.480
with engineers submitting
jobs in the early morning.

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:24.290
It ramps up during the day.

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:26.760
You'll notice there's a bit
of a lull there over the lunch

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:28.410
period.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.710
And then it tapers off
a bit towards midnight.

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:35.055
And we have some low-priority
jobs which can run overnight.

00:24:35.055 --> 00:24:36.430
But the majority
of our engineers

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:38.222
are getting their work
done during the day.

00:24:38.222 --> 00:24:40.330
And we don't want engineers
waiting on machines

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:42.700
if we can all avoid it.

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:45.610
So scaling like this is always
a balance between two things.

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:48.120
You have queue times
and utilization.

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:50.740
On the one hand, if we were
to scale up too quickly

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:52.810
or if we were to
scale down too slowly,

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:54.610
then we would end up
with excess capacity.

00:24:54.610 --> 00:24:56.652
And this would mean our
utilization would be low.

00:24:56.652 --> 00:24:58.910
We'd be paying for servers
that we don't need.

00:24:58.910 --> 00:25:01.480
On the other hand, if we
were to scale up too slowly

00:25:01.480 --> 00:25:04.570
or we scaled down
too quickly, then we

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:05.650
end up with jobs queuing.

00:25:05.650 --> 00:25:07.090
And that's not ideal
either, because now we're

00:25:07.090 --> 00:25:09.382
paying for engineers while
we're waiting for these jobs

00:25:09.382 --> 00:25:09.950
to finish.

00:25:09.950 --> 00:25:12.565
So we need to be able to
handle rapid changes in demand,

00:25:12.565 --> 00:25:14.440
but we also need to
ensure that we're closely

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:17.375
matching this demand
curve, and that we're not

00:25:17.375 --> 00:25:19.750
over or under provisioning
the number of servers we need.

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.810
We have some unique aspects
to managing this autoscaling

00:25:24.810 --> 00:25:26.170
at Cruise.

00:25:26.170 --> 00:25:28.920
So we need to balance the
scaling across multiple regions

00:25:28.920 --> 00:25:31.307
and zones, and instance groups.

00:25:31.307 --> 00:25:32.640
In some cases, we want to scale.

00:25:32.640 --> 00:25:36.180
We want to prefer scaling in one
region and only sort of start

00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:39.245
scaling up in another region
if we can't get capacity there.

00:25:39.245 --> 00:25:41.370
We have different queues
with different priorities.

00:25:41.370 --> 00:25:42.410
So if we have a
low-priority job,

00:25:42.410 --> 00:25:44.040
we might not want
to scale up at all.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:46.660
We're just happy for it to
kind of sit there and queue.

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:49.770
And we also need to avoid
interrupting long-running tasks

00:25:49.770 --> 00:25:52.010
when we're scaling down.

00:25:52.010 --> 00:25:54.750
And the default autoscale
that comes with the GCP

00:25:54.750 --> 00:25:56.770
can target something like
a stack driver metric.

00:25:56.770 --> 00:25:58.862
So you can target
something like CPU use,

00:25:58.862 --> 00:26:00.570
or you can target your
own custom metric,

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:02.760
like queue length.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:05.770
But unfortunately, when the
default autoscaler scales down,

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:07.780
it always picks a random
instance to terminate.

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:09.447
And when it does that,
it only gives you

00:26:09.447 --> 00:26:11.580
60 seconds notice
that this instance is

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:13.183
going to scale down.

00:26:13.183 --> 00:26:14.850
As I mentioned earlier,
if we're halfway

00:26:14.850 --> 00:26:17.540
through a 20 or
30-minute simulation job,

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:19.290
we really don't want
to kill that instance

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:22.190
just because it got unlucky.

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:25.430
So to meet these needs, we
built our own autoscaler.

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:27.870
And I highly recommend
this if you're not--

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:31.100
if the stock autoscaler
is not working for you.

00:26:31.100 --> 00:26:33.350
It's actually pretty easy
to build with just a few GCP

00:26:33.350 --> 00:26:34.410
components.

00:26:34.410 --> 00:26:36.260
So we have here a
Cloud Scheduler,

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:38.515
which kicks off a cloud
function every minute or so.

00:26:38.515 --> 00:26:40.640
And that cloud function is
just a few hundred lines

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:41.947
of JavaScript.

00:26:41.947 --> 00:26:44.030
So the cloud function is
doing a couple of things.

00:26:44.030 --> 00:26:46.970
It reaches out to Stackdriver,
and it asks, first of all,

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:47.700
for the demand.

00:26:47.700 --> 00:26:50.060
So how many jobs do we
have running in progress?

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:52.182
And how many jobs do we
currently have queued?

00:26:52.182 --> 00:26:54.140
And then it looks at the
supply side of things.

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:56.098
How many workers do we
currently have connected

00:26:56.098 --> 00:26:58.540
or that are
currently scaling up?

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.030
If it decides that we need to
scale up additional capacity,

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:03.220
it can do this really
simply by just reaching out

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:05.260
to the Instance Group
Manager API there,

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:07.810
and it can just say,
please set the target size

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.140
for this instance group to x.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:12.850
If it wants to scale down, it
puts a message on a Pub/Sub

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:14.760
queue, and then whenever
a worker is idle,

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.320
it simply checks
this queue to see

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:19.500
if there's a shutdown message
for that particular worker

00:27:19.500 --> 00:27:20.220
class.

00:27:20.220 --> 00:27:22.740
And the unlucky worker that
drew the short straw just

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:25.230
deregisters itself from
the Instance Group Manager,

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:28.540
and then it
terminates gracefully.

00:27:28.540 --> 00:27:30.400
So this architecture
allows us to ensure

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:35.273
that we're only ever terminating
workers that are idle.

00:27:35.273 --> 00:27:36.940
Another thing that
we needed to consider

00:27:36.940 --> 00:27:39.530
when running this many
instances is the networking.

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:42.150
So we run all of instances
in a private-- in a VPC,

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:44.880
and this uses
private IP addresses.

00:27:44.880 --> 00:27:46.660
And when you're using
private IPs, that

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:48.868
means that if you need to
reach the outside internet,

00:27:48.868 --> 00:27:50.610
then you need to
go through a NAT.

00:27:50.610 --> 00:27:53.130
Google has a product called
Cloud NAT, which went GA back

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:54.030
in November.

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:57.280
But when we set this up,
Cloud NAT was not available.

00:27:57.280 --> 00:28:00.090
So we actually had to manually
create a whole bunch of NATs.

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:02.490
And that meant we
needed to create

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:04.110
a NAT in every
availability zone,

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:05.340
basically, in every region.

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:07.920
And some availability zones
needed more than one NAT

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.775
to handle the bandwidth
throughput that we needed.

00:28:10.775 --> 00:28:12.650
And this works, but it's
still infrastructure

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:13.980
that we don't want to manage.

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:15.355
So we're currently
in the process

00:28:15.355 --> 00:28:16.760
of migrating to Cloud NAT.

00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:19.520
And Cloud NAT can automatically
scale horizontally

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:23.170
to support your demand.

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:25.920
Also, if you're doing any
communication with private--

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:28.090
with managed Google
services, you

00:28:28.090 --> 00:28:30.040
have to enable
Private Google Access.

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.620
So Private Google Access
allows your machines

00:28:32.620 --> 00:28:34.720
that are running on private
IP addresses to route

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:40.030
directly to those services like
BigQuery or GCS or Spanner.

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:42.370
It allows you to route
directly to those services

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:43.995
without needing to
go through your NAT.

00:28:43.995 --> 00:28:47.400
So it significantly reduces
the bottleneck on your NAT.

00:28:47.400 --> 00:28:49.590
Private Google Access is
actually pretty complicated

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:52.090
to set up if you are using--
if you're managing your own NAT

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:52.810
instances.

00:28:52.810 --> 00:28:54.870
But if you're using
Cloud NAT, it's

00:28:54.870 --> 00:28:56.700
a checkbox when you
set up your VPC.

00:28:56.700 --> 00:29:01.555
So check that box, and you
will live a happy life.

00:29:01.555 --> 00:29:03.930
The final picture of the--
the final piece of the picture

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:04.805
here is storage.

00:29:04.805 --> 00:29:06.930
So as I mentioned, we're
using Google Cloud Storage

00:29:06.930 --> 00:29:09.377
for storing inputs
and output data,

00:29:09.377 --> 00:29:11.460
and we're using Google
Container Registry to store

00:29:11.460 --> 00:29:13.625
our runtime container images.

00:29:13.625 --> 00:29:15.250
In Google Cloud
Storage, we have things

00:29:15.250 --> 00:29:18.280
like application binaries, map
tiles, machine learning models,

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:19.930
and sensor data,
all of these things

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:23.010
that we need to
run a simulation.

00:29:23.010 --> 00:29:25.290
We've gotten pretty amazing
throughput from GCS.

00:29:25.290 --> 00:29:26.760
So this is our write throughput.

00:29:26.760 --> 00:29:28.635
On a typical day, we
might be peeking at sort

00:29:28.635 --> 00:29:30.600
of a gigabyte per second.

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:33.090
But we don't write nearly
as much data as we read.

00:29:33.090 --> 00:29:34.680
So this is our read throughput.

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:37.080
And you can see on
a typical day we're

00:29:37.080 --> 00:29:40.330
peaking at over 380
gigabytes per second of data.

00:29:40.330 --> 00:29:41.880
So that's over 3 terabit.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:44.910
And this is an example of where
the cloud is super powerful.

00:29:44.910 --> 00:29:47.670
Because if we set out to
build infrastructure like this

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:49.560
ourselves, it
would be incredibly

00:29:49.560 --> 00:29:52.420
challenging to manage
reliably at that scale.

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:55.820
And it would also not be
value add work for Cruise.

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:58.280
Instead, we simply click
a button on the console

00:29:58.280 --> 00:30:00.050
or write a few
lines of Terraform,

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:02.120
and we have this kind of
capacity available to us

00:30:02.120 --> 00:30:05.172
instantly.

00:30:05.172 --> 00:30:07.630
We have a bunch of work coming
down the pipeline for Hydra.

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:10.120
So we're always making
performance improvements

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:11.930
to handle the increasing load.

00:30:11.930 --> 00:30:15.305
We're looking at task pipelining
so that we can prefetch assets

00:30:15.305 --> 00:30:17.680
for the next job that we're
about to run before we finish

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:19.840
the current job.

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:22.030
We're optimizing
where we send jobs

00:30:22.030 --> 00:30:25.120
based on which workers
already have resources cached.

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:26.830
And we're A/B testing
out worker updates

00:30:26.830 --> 00:30:30.757
so that we can make sure
that workers are working

00:30:30.757 --> 00:30:32.590
successfully, and that
we haven't introduced

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:34.960
any regressions in our
work infrastructure

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:38.262
before we start managing
production load.

00:30:38.262 --> 00:30:39.970
So the last thing I'm
going to talk about

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:43.580
is our data analytics pipeline.

00:30:43.580 --> 00:30:45.440
If you recall from
this previous slide,

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:49.590
I mentioned that we're storing
our output data in Google Cloud

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:50.090
Storage.

00:30:50.090 --> 00:30:52.260
And then we ingest
this into BigQuery.

00:30:52.260 --> 00:30:53.900
And like with
Hydra, I think it's

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:56.330
helpful to touch briefly on
the architecture of where

00:30:56.330 --> 00:30:59.260
we came from before I talk
about how we're using BigQuery.

00:30:59.260 --> 00:31:01.760
So in this diagram, you can see
our simulation JobScheduler.

00:31:01.760 --> 00:31:02.660
That's Hydra.

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:05.070
And Hydra is pulling
work from build artifacts

00:31:05.070 --> 00:31:07.342
and from other simulation
data that we need.

00:31:07.342 --> 00:31:09.050
And then the output
data that it produces

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:10.862
goes into our Graph
Compute Engine.

00:31:10.862 --> 00:31:12.320
And the Graph
Compute Engine allows

00:31:12.320 --> 00:31:14.240
engineers to run
post-processing tasks,

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:16.200
like they might want
to, for example,

00:31:16.200 --> 00:31:17.810
compare that output
data against human

00:31:17.810 --> 00:31:20.600
labeled ground truth data,
and create aggregations

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:25.680
on that to score how well
we did in that simulation.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:28.750
The post-processing data was
then stored Avro files in S3.

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:31.040
And we chose Avro here
for a couple of reasons.

00:31:31.040 --> 00:31:33.460
First of all, it was just
really easy to work with.

00:31:33.460 --> 00:31:36.870
Hydra already had the ability
to save Avro files into S3.

00:31:36.870 --> 00:31:39.320
And secondly, we didn't
need to worry about schemas.

00:31:39.320 --> 00:31:41.813
So our engineers could make
changes to the AV code,

00:31:41.813 --> 00:31:44.230
or they could make changes to
their post-processing steps.

00:31:44.230 --> 00:31:45.897
And that data would
get written directly

00:31:45.897 --> 00:31:47.620
to Avro files,
which they were then

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:50.087
able to access without
needing to go through any team

00:31:50.087 --> 00:31:51.670
or get approval to
change the schemas.

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:58.880
We had tools like Jupyter
Notebooks, and also

00:31:58.880 --> 00:32:01.378
custom analysis tooling,
which can directly

00:32:01.378 --> 00:32:03.170
read these Avro files,
and then render them

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:04.832
to create sorts of dashboards.

00:32:04.832 --> 00:32:06.290
But this architecture
became pretty

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:10.640
limiting once we started wanting
to run more advanced analysis.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:12.230
Without a proper
query engine here, we

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:15.410
couldn't answer advanced queries
without going back and editing

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:17.798
the post-processing code.

00:32:17.798 --> 00:32:19.965
And this also created pretty
significant memory use.

00:32:19.965 --> 00:32:21.830
Our front end
visualization tools

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:23.540
needed to read these Avro files.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.790
And it did not scale well
as the number of simulations

00:32:25.790 --> 00:32:27.492
increased.

00:32:27.492 --> 00:32:28.950
Finally, it's pretty
time consuming

00:32:28.950 --> 00:32:31.560
building this type of tooling
if you're tying it specifically

00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:33.707
to the data format
that you're using.

00:32:33.707 --> 00:32:35.290
There are many
different ways we could

00:32:35.290 --> 00:32:37.120
have gone about
solving this, but given

00:32:37.120 --> 00:32:38.800
that we were using
Google Cloud, we

00:32:38.800 --> 00:32:41.317
decided to use BigQuery
as the data warehouse.

00:32:41.317 --> 00:32:42.900
So we have an ETL
pipeline here that's

00:32:42.900 --> 00:32:44.932
a simple ingestion service.

00:32:44.932 --> 00:32:46.390
And that's taking
the data produced

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:48.820
by our post-processing
Graph Compute Engine,

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:51.855
and ingest this into BigQuery.

00:32:51.855 --> 00:32:53.730
We've had great success
with BigQuery so far.

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:55.820
So we're ingesting
over 10 terabytes

00:32:55.820 --> 00:32:59.200
a day of data with no
administration overhead to us.

00:32:59.200 --> 00:33:00.930
We can get this
data into BigQuery

00:33:00.930 --> 00:33:02.860
within minutes of
finishing a simulation.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:04.770
And we can query
it within seconds.

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:07.980
And the BigQuery SQL interface
is accessible and supported

00:33:07.980 --> 00:33:10.170
by many different
reporting tools, not just

00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:12.135
our in-house ones.

00:33:12.135 --> 00:33:14.260
Our engineers can access
this data via the BigQuery

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:16.450
console and UI.

00:33:16.450 --> 00:33:19.070
We also have things like
Jupyter Notebooks and BI

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:21.500
tools, like Looker and
Tableau, that can access it.

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:24.757
And we also have our own custom
front end analysis tooling.

00:33:24.757 --> 00:33:26.590
So the last thing I
want to talk about today

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:31.142
is a bit about this custom
analysis tooling that we built.

00:33:31.142 --> 00:33:33.600
I'll give you an insight into
the world of self-driving car

00:33:33.600 --> 00:33:34.492
development.

00:33:34.492 --> 00:33:36.450
So I'm going to walk
through a complete example

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:38.620
of a simulation
and how we run that

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.630
and how we understand
the results.

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:42.360
So here, bear with me here.

00:33:42.360 --> 00:33:44.610
We have a car trying to make
an unprotected left turn.

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:47.190
And the car needs to
yield to these cars

00:33:47.190 --> 00:33:48.700
in the oncoming lane of traffic.

00:33:48.700 --> 00:33:50.980
So this is represented in pink.

00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:53.380
We also have what we call a
region of interest, which is

00:33:53.380 --> 00:33:55.120
the dotted line that you see.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:56.860
And our car needs
to clear this region

00:33:56.860 --> 00:34:02.030
of interest between that gap in
traffic between those two cars.

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:04.030
What we're interested in
this particular example

00:34:04.030 --> 00:34:06.700
is which gap our car
chose to go through

00:34:06.700 --> 00:34:10.576
and what the gap size was,
what the period of time was.

00:34:10.576 --> 00:34:12.159
And the gap size is
the period of time

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:15.400
between when the car
one, the blue car,

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:17.090
has left that
region of interest,

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:18.940
and when car two, the
green car, entered

00:34:18.940 --> 00:34:21.510
that region of interest.

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:23.489
So if you're still with
me, keep that in mind.

00:34:23.489 --> 00:34:25.210
We'll move on to the next slide.

00:34:25.210 --> 00:34:27.600
This is an example of some
of the custom visualization

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:29.250
tooling that we
built. So you remember

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:32.340
we're looking at the gap
size between the two cars

00:34:32.340 --> 00:34:35.350
as we're making an
unprotected left hand.

00:34:35.350 --> 00:34:39.820
And in this particular example,
we're comparing two factors.

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:42.489
We're looking at how the
speed of those oncoming

00:34:42.489 --> 00:34:45.830
cars and the distance
between those oncoming cars

00:34:45.830 --> 00:34:49.310
affects the gap that we chose
and the size of that gap.

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:51.310
And you can see here that
we're comparing a base

00:34:51.310 --> 00:34:53.933
branch and a feature branch,
and then in the third box,

00:34:53.933 --> 00:34:56.350
you can see the delta between
the base and feature branch.

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:59.440
So engineers can easily identify
with this particular commit

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:01.750
how that impacted
which gap we chose

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:04.770
as a function of these
two input parameters.

00:35:04.770 --> 00:35:06.270
Each one of these
green squares here

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:09.510
is actually a separate
individual simulation.

00:35:09.510 --> 00:35:11.190
So that's running on Hydra.

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:14.135
And that's generating probably
a gigabyte of output data each.

00:35:14.135 --> 00:35:16.260
So you can see how we end
up with these large scale

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:17.820
compute and data
storage challenges

00:35:17.820 --> 00:35:20.043
once we start running
these types of simulations.

00:35:20.043 --> 00:35:22.710
Another thing about this tool is
that the engineers can actually

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:24.773
click on one of these
particular squares,

00:35:24.773 --> 00:35:26.190
and it gives them
more information

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:29.170
about that particular run.

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:32.130
So we have a lot of ongoing
work in the simulation

00:35:32.130 --> 00:35:32.880
tooling like this.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:34.755
We work closely with
our AV engineering teams

00:35:34.755 --> 00:35:37.732
to understand what type of
experiments they want to run

00:35:37.732 --> 00:35:39.690
and how we can best of
understand and visualize

00:35:39.690 --> 00:35:42.550
those experiments.

00:35:42.550 --> 00:35:44.312
So thank you for joining today.

00:35:44.312 --> 00:35:46.520
Before I go, I want to leave
you with three thoughts.

00:35:46.520 --> 00:35:49.010
So the first one, simulation
for self-driving cars

00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:51.090
is a super interesting problem.

00:35:51.090 --> 00:35:53.082
It's a privilege to
get to work on it.

00:35:53.082 --> 00:35:56.340
At Cruise, we're using the
latest tools and infrastructure

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:57.600
to build--

00:35:57.600 --> 00:35:59.380
to test these cars
at massive scale.

00:35:59.380 --> 00:36:01.710
So this includes things
like Google Cloud,

00:36:01.710 --> 00:36:03.843
and also in-house
developed software.

00:36:03.843 --> 00:36:05.760
And finally, it takes a
diverse range of skill

00:36:05.760 --> 00:36:07.862
sets to build self-driving cars.

00:36:07.862 --> 00:36:10.320
So we have not just machine
learning engineers and robotics

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:13.110
engineers, but back end
engineers, infrastructure

00:36:13.110 --> 00:36:16.935
engineers, 3D front end
engineers, data, data science.

00:36:16.935 --> 00:36:18.810
And we're hiring in San
Francisco and Seattle

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:21.227
if you're interested in working
on these types of problems

00:36:21.227 --> 00:36:22.400
every day.

00:36:22.400 --> 00:36:24.000
So thank you.

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:27.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

